  <h1>Why π Appears Between Zero and One</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Zero and one do not generate π by bare arithmetic. The exact statement is more
    interesting: once they bound a unit interval and that interval is given Euclidean closure, π is the
    normalising constant the geometry requires.</p>
</section>

{% include "partials/editorial-guide.njk" %}

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The claim, repaired before it is expanded</p>
    <h2 id="t-careful-claim">Not \(\{0,1\}\Rightarrow\pi\), but a Theorem with Declared Premises</h2>
    <p>The numerals 0 and 1 alone do not force π. Starting with them and applying finitely many additions,
      subtractions, multiplications and divisions produces rational numbers, while π is transcendental.
      Lindemann proved that transcendence in 1882. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>Something else must therefore enter. Here it is stated rather than hidden: choose the real interval
      \([0,1]\), give it the ordinary Euclidean metric, and ask for the measure associated with closing a
      straight diameter into a circle. Under those premises, π appears exactly. The dependency is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \{0,1\}
      +\text{continuum}
      +\text{Euclidean metric}
      +\text{closure}
      \;\Longrightarrow\;\pi,
      \qquad
      \text{not}\qquad
      \{0,1\}\;\Longrightarrow\;\pi.
    \]</p>
    <p>This distinction matters to TTI. The theorem earns the mathematics. Reading 0 and 1 as boundaries of
      existence, and requiring reality itself to close, remains the framework&rsquo;s speculative promotion.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established mathematics</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; TTI interpretation, not implied by the theorem</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The direct theorem on the unit interval</p>
    <h2 id="t-beta-theorem">Euler&rsquo;s Beta Integral Puts π Exactly Between 0 and 1</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The beta&ndash;gamma theorem states, for \(p,q&gt;0\),</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p class="formula">\[
        B(p,q)
        =\int_0^1 x^{p-1}(1-x)^{q-1}\,dx
        =\frac{\Gamma(p)\Gamma(q)}{\Gamma(p+q)}.
      \]</p>
      <p>Set both exponents to one half. Since \(\Gamma(1)=1\) and
        \(\Gamma(\tfrac12)=\sqrt{\pi}\), the theorem gives</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \boxed{
        \int_0^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}}
        =B\!\left(\frac12,\frac12\right)
        =\pi.}
      \]</p>
      <p>That is the most literal rigorous form of &ldquo;π from 0 and 1&rdquo; used here. The bounds are
        exactly 0 and 1. The kernel is symmetric under \(x\mapsto1-x\), so neither boundary is preferred.
        It diverges at both ends but is integrable: each edge opens into an infinite local slope while the
        complete passage between them has the finite measure π. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted">The endpoint infinities do not prove TTI&rsquo;s two-infinities cosmology. They provide
        an exact mathematical analogue with a finite total, which is why the theorem belongs in First
        Principles rather than being offered as physical evidence.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

