<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Why the beta theorem is circular geometry in interval form</p>
    <h2 id="t-change-variable">Open the Interval and the Semicircle Is Already There</h2>
    <p>Make the affine change of variable \(u=2x-1\). It carries \(x=0\) to \(u=-1\),
      \(x=1\) to \(u=1\), and gives</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      x(1-x)=\frac{1-u^2}{4},
      \qquad dx=\frac{du}{2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Therefore</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \int_0^1\frac{dx}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}}
      =\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{du}{\sqrt{1-u^2}}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Now take the upper unit semicircle \(y=\sqrt{1-u^2}\). Its arc-length element is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      ds=\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{dy}{du}\right)^2}\,du
      =\frac{du}{\sqrt{1-u^2}}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The beta integral is therefore the length of the upper semicircle of radius one: π. Equivalently,
      with \(u=\sin\theta\), the integral becomes
      \(\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}d\theta=\pi\). The apparently open interval is a diameter written in a
      coordinate that does not yet display its closing arc. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Normalisation, not numerology</p>
    <h2 id="t-arcsine-law">π Is the Constant That Makes the Whole Interval Equal One</h2>
    <p>Divide the beta kernel by its total. The result is the arcsine probability density:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      f(x)=\frac{1}{\pi\sqrt{x(1-x)}},
      \qquad 0&lt;x&lt;1,
      \qquad \int_0^1 f(x)\,dx=1.
    \]</p>
    <p>Its cumulative distribution is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      F(x)=\frac{2}{\pi}\arcsin\sqrt{x}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Here π has a precise job. Without the factor \(1/\pi\), the measure of the complete interval is π;
      with it, the measure is one. In that limited and exact sense, 0 and 1 set the bounds and π reconciles
      the divergent edge behaviour with a finite whole. No mystical property has been inserted into the
      calculation. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Two independent checks</p>
    <h2 id="t-checks">The Arctangent Route and Euler&rsquo;s Basel Theorem</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-arctangent">The fundamental theorem route</h3>
      <p>Because \(d(\arctan x)/dx=1/(1+x^2)\), the fundamental theorem of calculus gives</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        4\int_0^1\frac{dx}{1+x^2}
        =4\bigl(\arctan1-\arctan0\bigr)
        =\pi.
      \]</p>
      <p>Expanding the integrand for \(|x|&lt;1\), integrating to \(r&lt;1\), and taking
        \(r\to1^-\) by Abel&rsquo;s theorem yields the Gregory&ndash;Leibniz series:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \frac{\pi}{4}
        =1-\frac13+\frac15-\frac17+\cdots.
      \]</p>
      <p class="muted">The interval is again \([0,1]\), but this time π records a quarter-turn under
        stereographic slope rather than semicircular arc length.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-basel-theorem">The Basel theorem route</h3>
      <p>Euler&rsquo;s solution of the Basel problem proves</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{6},
        \qquad
        \pi=\sqrt{6\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}}.
      \]</p>
      <p>The sequence begins at one; its terms tend to zero; their infinite accumulation remains finite and
        recovers π. This is the theorem already used by TTI to control the infinite pole tail. It is an
        independent appearance of π in a bounded sum, not a derivation from the two numerals alone.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted">Read its existing stress-test application at
        <a href="/stress-tests/#t-basel">The Infinite Poles, and the Basel Rescue</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The geometry has to be named</p>
    <h2 id="t-non-euclidean">Why Closure Alone Is Not Enough</h2>
    <p>TTI previously said that closure requires π. That sentence is too compressed. Closed paths exist in
      spherical, hyperbolic and many other geometries. On a curved surface, the circumference of a geodesic
      circle is not generally \(2\pi r\); it depends on curvature and scale. Topological closure by itself
      does not select the Euclidean circle or its metric.</p>
    <p>The defensible version is narrower: <strong>normalised Euclidean circular closure introduces π</strong>.
      If TTI claims that the physical universe must inherit that relation at a foundational boundary, it must
      justify the metric rather than smuggling it in with the word &ldquo;circle&rdquo;. This page makes that
      research debt visible. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

