<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The proof in plain language</p>
    <h2 id="t-worked-reading">Walk Through the Interval Without Skipping a Step</h2>
    <p>Begin with every real position strictly between 0 and 1. The factor
      \(1/\sqrt{x(1-x)}\) gives positions near either endpoint more weight than positions near the middle.
      The weight grows without bound as \(x\) approaches 0 or 1, but it does so gently enough that the total
      remains finite. This is an <em>integrable singularity</em>: the graph has infinitely high ends without
      enclosing infinite area.</p>
    <p>Now describe a point in that interval by an angle rather than by its horizontal position. Set
      \(x=\sin^2\theta\), where \(0&lt;\theta&lt;\pi/2\). As θ moves through one quarter-turn, \(x\) moves once
      from 0 to 1. Differentiation gives \(dx=2\sin\theta\cos\theta\,d\theta\), while
      \(\sqrt{x(1-x)}=\sin\theta\cos\theta\) on this range. The apparently difficult endpoint factors
      therefore cancel:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac{dx}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}}
      =\frac{2\sin\theta\cos\theta\,d\theta}
      {\sin\theta\cos\theta}=2\,d\theta.
    \]</p>
    <p>The whole weighted interval is consequently just twice a quarter-turn. Its measure is
      \(2(\pi/2-0)=\pi\). Nothing has been estimated, fitted or read from a circle with a ruler. The circle
      enters through the trigonometric substitution, and the integral proves that this angular description
      and the original 0-to-1 description have exactly the same total.</p>
    <p class="lede">Zero and one supply the limits; endpoint symmetry supplies the kernel; Euclidean angle
      supplies the substitution; integration supplies the total. Remove any one of those ingredients and the
      conclusion no longer follows in this form.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap reveal">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Two equally useful readings</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-readings">π as a Length, and π as a Normalising Constant</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>The geometric reading</h3>
      <p>Use \(u=2x-1\) to stretch the unit interval into the diameter \(-1&lt;u&lt;1\). The same integral becomes
        \(\int_{-1}^{1}du/\sqrt{1-u^2}\). Its integrand is the derivative of \(\arcsin u\), so the total is
        the angular sweep from \(-\pi/2\) to \(\pi/2\): one semicircle. Here π measures closure directly.</p>
      <p>This does not mean that a straight interval secretly has the shape of a semicircle. It means that a
        lawful change of coordinate reveals a circular metric already encoded by the chosen square-root
        relation. Coordinates change; the integral does not.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="frame card">
      <h3>The probabilistic reading</h3>
      <p>Divide the same kernel by π and it becomes the arcsine probability density,
        \(p(x)=1/[\pi\sqrt{x(1-x)}]\). A probability density must total exactly 1, so π is the unique
        normalising constant that makes the entire interval one complete distribution.</p>
      <p>This reading is especially close to TTI&rsquo;s language. The open interval has extreme weight near
        both boundaries, yet neither boundary is an event with positive probability. Completion belongs to
        the integral over the relation, not to either endpoint by itself. The resemblance is structural; it
        is not evidence that the universe literally samples an arcsine distribution.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">What the theorem settles</p>
    <h2 id="t-zero-one-roles">What Zero and One Actually Do</h2>
    <p>Zero marks one boundary and one marks the other. Arithmetic formed only from those two numerals by a
      finite list of rational operations cannot produce π, because π is transcendental. The theorem succeeds
      for a different reason: 0 and 1 define a continuum, the symmetric kernel relates every interior point
      to both ends, and the integral sums that relation. “From zero and one” therefore means <em>from a
      structure bounded by zero and one</em>, not from concatenating or manipulating two digits.</p>
    <p>This distinction matters beyond mathematical etiquette. If a proposed physics merely notices π in a
      formula, it has explained nothing. To do explanatory work it must identify the physical quantity that
      ranges over the interval, justify the endpoint weighting, and show why a Euclidean angular coordinate
      is appropriate. TTI has supplied an exact mathematical model and an ontological analogy; it has not yet
      supplied that physical bridge.</p>
    <p>A useful test is to alter a premise. Replace the exponents one-half in the beta integral by other
      positive values and the answer is generally a different beta-function value. Replace Euclidean closure
      by another geometry and circumference is no longer governed globally by the flat-space ratio. The
      appearance of π is therefore neither arbitrary nor automatic. It records precisely the symmetry and
      geometry that were declared.</p>
    <p>For a reader who remembers only one line, it should be this:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \boxed{\displaystyle
      \int_0^1 \frac{dx}{\sqrt{x(1-x)}}=\pi}
      \quad\text{is a theorem; its use as a creation model is a hypothesis.}
    \]</p>
    <p>A numerical check can make the result tangible, provided the singular endpoints are handled honestly.
      Sampling the original integrand with equally spaced rectangles converges awkwardly because the graph
      rises sharply at both ends. Sampling after \(x=\sin^2\theta\) is simple: the transformed integrand is
      the constant 2 over a quarter-turn. This is not a different proof or a trick that hides infinity. It is
      the practical value of choosing coordinates adapted to the geometry.</p>
    <p>The example also explains why an improper integral may be finite. One evaluates from
      \(\varepsilon\) to \(1-\varepsilon\) and then lets \(\varepsilon\) approach zero. The endpoint values
      themselves are never inserted into the denominator. What matters is the finite limiting area of every
      interior contribution. Thus 0 and 1 remain boundaries rather than ordinary sampled points.</p>
    <p class="muted">That sentence protects both sides of the project. It prevents the speculative reading
      from borrowing certainty it has not earned, and it prevents an exact result from being dismissed merely
      because the project asks a larger philosophical question.</p>
  </div>
</section>

